## Founding & History
Craigie Knowe Vineyard was established in 1979 by John Austwick, a Hobart dentist. It holds the distinction of being the oldest vineyard on Tasmania's East Coast. Austwick was considered a pioneer, initially planting Bordeaux Blend grapes including Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, and Petite Verdot, and later expanding to include Pinot Noir and Riesling. He crafted his wines on-site in the building that now serves as the cellar door.

Austwick sold the vineyard in 2009. The property was subsequently left unattended for two vintages in 2011 and 2012 by a later owner. Glenn and Sandy Travers purchased the winery in 2013. They undertook a comprehensive makeover, which involved planting new vines and transforming the original winery into a rustic tasting facility. The historic homestead on the property, built in 1836 or circa 1842, is recognized as one of Tasmania's most photographed houses.

## People
Craigie Knowe Vineyard and Cellar Door is a family-owned establishment operated by father and son, Glenn and Alex Travers. Sandy Travers is also noted as a purchaser in 2013. Alex Travers is involved in vineyard management and has undertaken studies in Viticulture. The vineyard manager is Tristan Gebecki. Alex's wife also contributes to the team, and Glenn and Sandy's daughter, Kate, assists on weekends and with social media. Sandy's father, Alan, helps in the vineyard and crafts tasting boards.

The wine is crafted off-site by winemaker Matt Wood of Spring Vale. However, one source also lists Barry Kooij as being in charge of winemaking.

## Vineyard & Site
The vineyard is situated in Cranbrook on Tasmania's East Coast. The property spans 20 hectares, with 5 hectares under vine. The site benefits from a unique micro-climate and rich volcanic soils.

Original plantings by John Austwick included Bordeaux Blend varietals: Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, and Petite Verdot. The vineyard has since expanded its offerings to include Pinot Noir, Riesling, Chardonnay, and Pinot Gris. In 2017, Craigie Knowe also leased the Glen Heriot site, located less than 2 kilometers away, where they cultivate Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, and additional Pinot Noir.

The farming approach emphasizes meticulous vineyard management, guided by a viticulturist, and a philosophy of growing exceptional fruit. Practices include manual pruning and hand harvesting to ensure quality. The winery is committed to sustainable practices and small-batch production.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door is open Monday through Sunday from 11:00 AM to 4:00 PM. However, one source indicates hours of 10:00 AM to 5:00 PM on Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, and Sunday, with Wednesday closed.

Visitors can enjoy seated tastings of the current release wines, which are guided by one of the owners and provide insights into the vineyard's history and management practices. The cellar door offers both normal tastings and a decadent chocolate and wine matching experience. The tasting room is described as rustic yet elegant, with ample seating both indoors and outdoors among the vines. The vineyard encourages visitors to explore the tranquil landscape. The cellar door is dog-friendly, though guests are asked to check in first for a supervised meet and greet with the resident dogs.

## Food & Hospitality
Flora's Restaurant is located on-site. The menu, created by a head chef with 15 years of experience in France, focuses on modern Australian cuisine and celebrates local ingredients. The restaurant offers gourmet vineyard plates, cheese boards, and wine-tasting platters, thoughtfully paired with local artisanal cheeses and Tasmanian chocolates.

During summer, the restaurant is generally open daily from 11:30 AM to 3:00 PM. It is also open for lunch Wednesday through Sunday and for dinner on Saturday nights from 6:00 PM. The venue is equipped to host functions and has also hosted live music events, featuring acts such as the Wolfe Brothers and Ash Grunwald. Craigie Knowe also provides camping facilities for fully self-contained campers.

## Awards & Recognition
Craigie Knowe Vineyard received the customer service gong at the 2024 Tasmanian Community Achievement Awards. It boasts an impressive 4.9-star rating from nearly 300 reviews. French reviewer Nicolas Biar once described Tasmania as having "one of wine's most beautiful futures" in a context related to Craigie Knowe.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Craigie Knowe Vineyard is committed to sustainable practices and small-batch production. Their philosophy involves meticulous vineyard management and growing exceptional fruit to enable minimal intervention winemaking. No specific organic or biodynamic certifications were found in the public sources.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Craigie Knowe Vineyard is 80 Glen Gala Road, Cranbrook, TAS 7190, Australia. It is approximately 1.5 hours from either Launceston or Hobart. Swansea is 16 kilometers away, and Bicheno is 26 kilometers away. Freycinet National Park is a 40-minute drive.

The phone number is 0499 901 109. The official website is http://craigieknowe.com.au/. Visitors are encouraged to follow their Instagram or Facebook pages for updates, with their Instagram handle being @craigieknowevineyard. The vineyard welcomes fully self-contained campers.

## Notable / Distinctive
Craigie Knowe Vineyard holds the distinction of being the oldest vineyard on Tasmania's East Coast. Its founding by John Austwick in 1979 was a pioneering venture, met with initial skepticism from local farmers. The property's homestead, built in 1836 or circa 1842, is considered one of Tasmania's most photographed houses.

The vineyard is a family-owned and operated business, managed by the father-and-son team of Glenn and Alex Travers. Beyond wine, Craigie Knowe also produces the "Bluff Spirits" range, which includes vodkas and pre-mixed cans. The on-site Flora's Restaurant features a head chef with 15 years of experience gained in France. The vineyard aims to become a destination for live music events.

